  (RUNTIME-EXCEPTION.md).
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  lua.pcpp - the Lua unit: `import lua;` embeds Lua (a small, fast scripting
  language) in a PaskalCPP program. A self-describing vendor module: it owns
  every include path, library path and link library it needs, per official
  target. The library is liblua.a (one per target: the Lua core and its
  standard libraries), built by PaskalCPP-Libs' build-lua.cmd from the
  official release, linked by @linklibrary: every module of the program
  shares the one copy, and a program links only the libraries it imports.

  The API is Lua's C API as it ships (https://www.lua.org/manual/5.5/), called
  straight from PaskalCPP through lua.hpp: luaL_newstate gives a
  `ptr to lua_State`, luaL_openlibs opens the standard libraries,
  luaL_loadstring / luaL_loadfile compile a chunk and lua_pcall runs it
  (LUA_OK, or an error code with the message on top of the stack), and
  lua_close frees the state. Values cross through Lua's stack: lua_push* put
  them on, lua_to* / luaL_check* read them, lua_getglobal / lua_setglobal
  reach the globals, lua_getfield the fields of a table.

  A PaskalCPP routine Lua can call is a `routine clink Name(AL: ptr to
  lua_State): int32` (a C function, as lua_CFunction is): it reads its
  arguments from the stack, pushes its results and returns how many it
  pushed; lua_register(AL, "name", Name) makes it a global. Strings cross as
  UTF-8 C strings: a PaskalCPP string's .c_str() goes in, lua_tostring gives
  `ptr to const char`.

  Lua's errors are C longjmp's (the library is compiled as C): lua_error,
  luaL_error and a failing luaL_check* jump out of the routine that calls
  them, past the C++ destructors of anything that routine or its callers
  between there and the lua_pcall hold. A routine Lua calls therefore checks
  its arguments before it makes any value that needs freeing (a string, an
  array), and returns rather than raises once it holds one. Run chunks with
  lua_pcall (or luaL_dostring), never lua_call, so an error comes back as a
  code rather than ending the program.
==============================================================================*/

module unit lua;

@addincludepath "$P:res/libs/vendor/lua/include";

// Lua needs the C runtime: on Windows mingw's CRT (linked by default), on
// Linux libc, libm (the math library) and libdl (require of C modules)
@ifdef TARGET_X86_64_WINDOWS_GNU
  @addlibrarypath "$P:res/libs/vendor/lua/lib/x86_64-windows-gnu";
@elseif TARGET_X86_64_LINUX_GNU
  @addlibrarypath "$P:res/libs/vendor/lua/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu";
  @linklibrary "m";
  @linklibrary "dl";
@else
  @message error "lua: unsupported target";
@endif

@linklibrary "lua";

// The header goes to this unit's header, so every importer sees Lua's API;
// lua.hpp wraps Lua's C headers in extern "C"
cpp header;

#include <lua.hpp>

end.
